"Reveals a defined pattern of informal relationships among people or teams beyond their formal reporting hierarchy.\n\nUse when: Find collaboration bridges, isolated groups or overloaded advice sources. Compare formal structure with observed information exchange.\n\nAvoid when: Relationship observations are incomplete or too sensitive for the intended audience.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes represent people or teams at a consistent aggregation level. Edges encode one explicitly defined relationship and observation period. Direction or strength appears only when the source evidence supports it.\n\nBuild: Choose a relationship question such as who seeks advice from whom. Collect appropriately scoped observations and explain missing coverage. Create focused community views or a matrix when dense relationships would cross.\n\nCheck: Ties use a consistent definition and direction. Missing observations are distinguished from confirmed absence. Use separate views instead of deleting relationships to eliminate crossings.\n\nPitfalls: Equating a central position with overall job performance. Interpreting an unobserved relationship as evidence of no collaboration.\n\nExample: A volunteer organization maps advice requests and discovers that one coordinator bridges two teams, prompting additional cross-team introductions.\n\nAlternatives: Organization chart, Service ecosystem map."
